20260529.112520.188 [<ideas.........] # MBC2: Three boundary explorations for the clippy fix prompt
20260529.112520.196 [<ideas.........] The formal system here is unchanged: FAISS approximate neighbor pools, deterministic distance-then-index tie-breaking,
20260529.112520.227 [<ideas.........] optional escalation to a full training-row scan, and `ENNSurrogate` incremental fitting over `EpistemicNearestNeighbors`.
20260529.112520.867 [<ideas.........] The clippy failures are symptoms of how that system is *organized*, not invitations to add `#[allow(...)]` or one-line
20260529.112521.406 [<ideas.........] signature tweaks. Below are three structurally distant resolutions that stay inside those rules.
20260529.112521.425 [<ideas.........] ---
20260529.112521.472 [<ideas.........] ## Core assumptions (shared)
20260529.112521.759 [<ideas.........] | Assumption | Extreme limit | Negation | Pathological edge |
20260529.112521.905 [<ideas.........] |---|---|---|---|
20260529.112522.668 [<ideas.........] | Tie-break is a flat library of stateless functions | Every decision is a separate exported predicate | One monolithic
20260529.112523.042 [<ideas.........] mutating pass owns all branches | `k=0`, `n_train=k`, all distances equal, pool saturated at cutoff |
20260529.112523.620 [<ideas.........] | `faiss_pool_might_need_escalation` is an independent probe | Probe runs on every query before any pool mutation | Probe
20260529.112524.064 [<ideas.........] does not exist; logic lives only inside finalize | Probe true but finalize cheaply resolves without row scan |
20260529.112525.043 [<ideas.........] | `finalize_faiss_pool_topk` receives all context as loose parameters | Eight or more parameters per call | Zero free
20260529.112525.063 [<ideas.........] parameters; caller holds a session object | Batch of 10⁵ queries re-allocating scratch every iteration |
20260529.112525.663 [<ideas.........] | `ENNSurrogate` fittedness is three independent `Option`s | Each field checked separately with `expect` | Fitted state is a
20260529.112526.005 [<ideas.........] single indivisible bundle | `model` set, `fitter` missing after partial failure |
20260529.112526.755 [<ideas.........] | Helpers take `&mut Vec` because pairs grow/shrink | Every signature uses growable buffers | Every signature uses `&mut
20260529.112527.148 [<ideas.........] [(f64, i64)]` only | In-place sort on empty or length-1 slice |
20260529.112527.193 [<ideas.........] ---
20260529.112527.497 [<ideas.........] ## Idea 1: Make escalation the scheduler, not an orphan predicate
20260529.112528.088 [<ideas.........] **Structural move:** Invert control in `neighbor.rs` so `faiss_pool_might_need_escalation` becomes the *first* branch in the
20260529.112528.573 [<ideas.........] per-query loop, not dead code tested in isolation while production always enters `finalize_faiss_pool_topk`.
20260529.112529.030 [<ideas.........] **Assumption pushed:** The escalation probe is cheap enough to run unconditionally before any pool mutation. At the extreme,
20260529.112529.677 [<ideas.........] every query pays one read-only scan of sorted `(distance, index)` pairs; queries with distinct cutoffs never touch
20260529.112529.934 [<ideas.........] `try_resolve_boundary_tie_in_pool`, row-distance caches, or cutoff-band sorts.
20260529.112530.615 [<ideas.........] **Negation explored:** Finalize remains the only entry point, but the probe is *inlined privately* inside it (opposite pole:
20260529.112531.055 [<ideas.........] delete the public function entirely). Idea 1 takes the opposite negation: elevate the probe to production control flow.
20260529.112531.539 [<ideas.........] **Pathological cases:** When `tie_break_neighbors` is false, the probe short-circuits to false and the loop becomes
20260529.112532.075 [<ideas.........] truncate-only. When the pool has a boundary tie but `below + at_cut ≤ k`, the probe may return true while finalize still
20260529.112532.700 [<ideas.........] resolves without row scan—so the scheduler must treat “might need escalation” as “enter finalize,” not “skip straight to
20260529.112532.720 [<ideas.........] full row scan.”
20260529.112532.950 [<ideas.........] **What changes structurally (not a parameter tweak):**
20260529.112533.550 [<ideas.........] - `neighbor.rs` gains an explicit three-phase loop: build sorted pairs → read-only escalation check → either cheap truncate
20260529.112533.799 [<ideas.........] or full finalize/scan path.
20260529.112534.273 [<ideas.........] - `dead_code` disappears because the function becomes part of the hot path semantics, not a test-only export.
20260529.112534.870 [<ideas.........] - `ptr_arg` and `too_many_arguments` on finalize may remain for the expensive branch only, shrinking how often that
20260529.112534.967 [<ideas.........] signature is exercised.
20260529.112534.997 [<ideas.........] ---
20260529.112535.365 [<ideas.........] ## Idea 2: Replace the function soup with a `FaissPoolTieBreakSession`
20260529.112536.044 [<ideas.........] **Structural move:** Introduce one batch-scoped session struct (e.g. `FaissPoolTieBreakSession<'a>`) that owns everything
20260529.112536.725 [<ideas.........] `finalize_faiss_pool_topk` currently receives as separate arguments: references to the model and query row, `k`, flags,
20260529.112536.875 [<ideas.........] optional precomputed row distances, pool size, and reusable scratch (`below`, `at_cut`, row-distance cache).
20260529.112537.543 [<ideas.........] **Assumption pushed:** Tie-breaking is not a set of free functions but a *phase* with persistent scratch across queries in
20260529.112538.175 [<ideas.........] the same batch. At the asymptotic limit (many query rows, fixed training set), scratch vectors are allocated once per batch
20260529.112538.684 [<ideas.........] and cleared per row instead of passing `&mut (Vec, Vec)` through an eight-argument call site.
20260529.112539.416 [<ideas.........] **Negation explored:** No session object at all—finalize stays a single function but returns an enum (`ResolvedInPool` |
20260529.112540.144 [<ideas.........] `NeedFullRowScan`) and mutates only `pairs: &mut [(f64, i64)]`. Idea 2 negates the “every call carries full context” pattern
20260529.112540.506 [<ideas.........] in the opposite direction: context lives in the session, not the call.
20260529.112541.216 [<ideas.........] **Pathological cases:** A session reused across rows must reset caches so row *i*’s lazily computed distances never leak
20260529.112541.906 [<ideas.........] into row *i+1*. The session’s internal API uses `&mut [(f64, i64)]` for in-place cutoff-band operations
20260529.112542.211 [<ideas.........] (`resolve_pool_tie_break_at_cutoff`, `apply_index_tie_break_at_cutoff`), which directly addresses `ptr_arg` without merely
20260529.112542.302 [<ideas.........] renaming parameters.
20260529.112542.347 [<ideas.........] **What changes structurally:**
20260529.112542.872 [<ideas.........] - `too_many_arguments` and `type_complexity` (the scratch tuple) collapse into fields on the session; the public call
20260529.112543.103 [<ideas.........] becomes `session.finalize_or_escalate(pairs) -> bool`.
20260529.112544.223 [<ideas.........] - `neighbor.rs` constructs one session before the query loop and reuses it, which is a different shape from today’s `(Vec,
20260529.112544.710 [<ideas.........] Vec)` tuple allocated beside the loop.
20260529.112544.730 [<ideas.........] - Helper functions become private methods or module-private fns taking `&mut Session` plus `&mut [(f64, i64)]`, not eight
20260529.112544.750 [<ideas.........] parallel parameters.
20260529.112544.768 [<ideas.........] ---
20260529.112544.948 [<ideas.........] ## Idea 3: Collapse `ENNSurrogate`’s three `Option`s into one `FittedState` (or typestate)
20260529.112545.383 [<ideas.........] **Structural move:** Replace `model: Option<_>`, `params: Option<_>`, and `fitter: Option<_>` with a single `fitted:
20260529.112545.788 [<ideas.........] Option<FittedState>` where `FittedState { model, params, fitter }` is the only representable post-fit configuration.
20260529.112546.335 [<ideas.........] Refactor `run_fitter`, `fit_append_internal`, and `fit` so each branches once on `self.fitted.as_mut()` instead of chaining
20260529.112546.626 [<ideas.........] `is_some()` with `expect("model")`.
20260529.112547.258 [<ideas.........] **Assumption pushed:** Fittedness is an atomic unit; you never have a model without the fitter state machine that maintains
20260529.112547.754 [<ideas.........] params. At the extreme, invalid partial states are unrepresentable, so clippy’s `unnecessary unwrap` pattern cannot recur at
20260529.112547.794 [<ideas.........] every layer.
20260529.112548.508 [<ideas.........] **Negation explored (more distant pole):** Typestate split—`ENNSurrogate` before fit vs `FittedENNSurrogate` after `fit`
20260529.112549.135 [<ideas.........] returns—so `fit_append` exists only on the fitted type and the cold-start path in `fit_append_internal` (model absent,
20260529.112549.769 [<ideas.........] create model + fitter together) becomes a constructor on the unfitted type. No runtime `Option` check remains in append
20260529.112549.787 [<ideas.........] logic.
20260529.112550.191 [<ideas.........] **Pathological cases:** Incremental `fit` that rejects prefix mismatch must drop the entire `FittedState` and rebuild, not
20260529.112550.834 [<ideas.........] clear fields independently. `run_fitter` when `fitter` is absent but `model` exists (today possible transiently in
20260529.112551.426 [<ideas.........] `fit_append_internal`’s cold path) must be re-expressed as “create `FittedState` in one assignment” rather than three
20260529.112551.484 [<ideas.........] separate `self.* = Some(...)`.
20260529.112551.526 [<ideas.........] **What changes structurally:**
20260529.112552.148 [<ideas.........] - The clippy fix is not `if let Some(model) = self.model.as_mut()` (that would be a small local variation); it is
20260529.112552.679 [<ideas.........] eliminating the redundant proof obligation by having one match arm bind `model`, `fitter`, and `params` together.
20260529.112553.179 [<ideas.........] - `fit_append_internal`’s two paths (“already have model” vs “cold start”) become either two methods on different state
20260529.112553.752 [<ideas.........] representations or two explicit transitions on `fitted: Option<FittedState>`, which reads differently from the current
20260529.112553.887 [<ideas.........] nested `Option` lattice.
20260529.112553.905 [<ideas.........] ---
20260529.112553.933 [<ideas.........] ## tl;dr
20260529.112554.370 [<ideas.........] 1. **Escalation scheduler:** Wire `faiss_pool_might_need_escalation` into `neighbor.rs` as the read-only gate before
20260529.112554.603 [<ideas.........] finalize runs, so dead code becomes production control flow instead of a test-only export.
20260529.112555.081 [<ideas.........] 2. **Session object:** Replace the eight-argument `finalize_faiss_pool_topk` and scratch tuple with a reusable
20260529.112555.454 [<ideas.........] `FaissPoolTieBreakSession` that holds context and buffers and operates on `&mut [(f64, i64)]` slices.
20260529.112555.878 [<ideas.........] 3. **Atomic fitted state:** Merge `ENNSurrogate`’s three `Option` fields into one `FittedState` (or a typestate split), so
20260529.112556.533 [<ideas.........] `fit_append_internal` never uses `is_some()` plus `expect` to recover a model that was just checked.
